WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.470
- Climate is so important that, you know,

00:00:03.470 --> 00:00:08.126
I'm dedicated to the cause and
whatever obstacles there are.

00:00:08.126 --> 00:00:13.043
I'll keep finding new ways to
try and move this innovation.

00:00:15.079 --> 00:00:19.552
It would be tragic to not
have that demonstration money.

00:00:19.552 --> 00:00:21.650
Critical for all these
climate technologies

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:26.650
is to get the cost down and
to be able to scale them up

00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:30.180
to a pretty gigantic level.

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:33.120
You'll never get that scale up

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:36.170
unless the government's coming
in with the right policies

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:39.580
and for the top areas, the right policy

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:42.320
is exactly what's in
that infrastructure bill.

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:45.380
What's great in this bill is this has

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:50.230
a demonstration funding over 25 billion,

00:00:50.230 --> 00:00:52.550
until you do the demonstration project,

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:55.540
you cannot get to a market driven price.

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:57.870
And the goal of the demonstration projects

00:00:57.870 --> 00:01:02.870
is to drop the green premium
over time, all the way to zero.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:05.950
So that using clean hydrogen eventually,

00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:09.520
as part of how you make steel for example,

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:12.560
the market alone makes that effective.

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:14.890
And so the government and innovation

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:17.730
play this dramatic role,
not just for the US,

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:19.223
but for the entire world.

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:23.164
- These big leaps forward

00:01:23.164 --> 00:01:25.824
to demonstrate that we can actually do it.

00:01:25.824 --> 00:01:27.790
So what this shows, this leadership shows

00:01:27.790 --> 00:01:30.690
is that the private sector
is hungry to partner,

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:33.130
is hungry to get in the game on this.

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:37.470
And we're hoping that
this example sparks others

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:39.320
to also step up and make sure

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:41.510
that we can get these
demonstration projects

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:44.630
fully funded and in the
ground and going quickly,

00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:46.860
it really helps to grease the skids

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:48.090
to make sure it happens quickly,

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:50.507
'cause we are in a moment of urgency.

00:01:50.507 --> 00:01:53.090
(gentle music)

00:01:55.910 --> 00:01:58.710
- What happens if the
bill doesn't get passed?

00:01:58.710 --> 00:01:59.610
There are still questions

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:01.590
about what the house is going to do.

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:03.770
In that case, do you walk away?

00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:08.480
- I would be super disappointed
if the bill doesn't pass,

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:11.240
I will continue to share
that with politicians

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:14.380
of both parties, but the focus

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:16.310
for large demonstration projects

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:19.311
will be outside the United States.

00:02:19.311 --> 00:02:24.311
Much more in Europe and Asia
than in the United States,

00:02:24.490 --> 00:02:27.490
which means you don't
get the direct connection

00:02:27.490 --> 00:02:31.040
with the huge innovation
power of the United States.

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:34.580
You don't get that early
lead in these industries,

00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:38.890
which often determines where
the long-term job creation is.

00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:43.890
I'll keep finding the ways
to draw in the US innovation.

